 Prisoner’s dilemma
I Two suspects in a major crime are held in separate cells. There is enough evidence to convict each of them of a minor offense, but not enough evidence to convict either of them of the major crime unless one of them acts as an informer against the other (confess).
I If they both stay quiet (deny), each will be convicted of the minor offense and spend one year in prison.
I If one and only one of them confesses, she will be freed and used as a witness against the other, who will spend four years in prison. If they both confess, each will spend three years in prison.


